  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1235 AM CDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\nWe're about 6C cooler at 850mb today compared to yesterday, with \nlighter and less organized low level flow. Thus will stick with NBM \nfor highs today despite its underperformance over the last 48 hours. \n\nA mid level warm advection push may provide for some elevated \ninstability Thursday. NBM has some low POPs/mention of rain showers \nin central South Dakota. More probable is a mid level (5kft) cloud \ndeck, and even thats just been removed from the latest RRFS BUFKIT \nprofiles. Warm air advection continues into Friday, and as with most \nwarm advection regimes, confidence on timing how warm it gets aloft \n48+ hours from now has some uncertainty. The 25th-75th percentile \nrange in high temperatures is 5 to 8 degrees for Friday.\n\nLatest GEFS takes a significant leap in comparison to 24 hours \nago, well into 2 standard deviations above climo with a mean of \n+24C at 850mb. With about a 4C increase, this would lend greater\nconfidence in \"just shy of\" record temperatures for Saturday. \nCurrent NBM however, falls short of recorded by about 5 to 10 \ndegrees. The 25th to 75th percentile range is generally low for \nSaturday, though north central South Dakota range is 7 to 10F as\na cold front may or may not be moving in thereabouts. \n\nThe strength/timing of the cold advection that follows is also quite \nuncertain, with a 15 to 21 degree range in 25th-75th percentile \ntemperatures Sunday. \n\nAs for precipitation potential, some changes in regards to the upper \npattern which is transitioning to more of a split flow outcome in \nthe GFS/EC, with a cut off low over 4 corners region with a northern \nstream wave that passes overhead still in a northwest flow mode. The \nresult is while we'll still see the cooler air, the rather broad low \npressure system across the Northern Rockies then passes into the \nNorthern Plains. This more west to east trajectory and faster \nprogression ends up locking us out of the better moisture \nreturn and leaves us with wrap around light rain on the \nbackside.",
